.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAP-DEPLOY-INFRASTRUCTURE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eap-deploy-infrastructure \- store an application's declaration beside its artifact and apply it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eap deploy-infrastructure
.BR \-\-application\-id | \-n
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-dir | \-d
.IR FOLDER ]
.RB [ \-\-dry\-run ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Merges an application's declaration folder into one document, stores it in the
application's own bucket as
.IR <application-id>.euclid.json ,
and asks EAP to apply it.
.SH THE DECLARATION
An application's own repository carries a
.I euclid/
folder: one file naming the queues and topics it owns, another the buckets
somebody else owns that it has to reach.
.PP
.RS
.nf
{ "version": 1,
  "creates": { "queues": [ {"name": "file-logging-cancel-queue"} ] },
  "uses":    { "buckets": [ {"name": "transfer-server",
                             "access": ["subscribe", "read"],
                             "owner": "ftp-server"} ] } }
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B creates
is what the application owns: euclid makes these.
.B uses
is what somebody else owns: euclid grants access and creates nothing, because a
service that could conjure another team's queue by naming it would make
ownership meaningless. A
.B uses
entry naming something absent is refused.
.PP
How the files are split is the author's business \(em they are merged by section
and kind, not by filename. Two of them naming the same resource is refused
rather than resolved: whichever a merge preferred, the other author would have
been overruled without being told.
.SH WHY BESIDE THE ARTIFACT
A jar cannot be read without unpacking it, and a declaration euclid has to open
the artifact to find is one no operator can look at either. As a sibling object
it is fetched, diffed and versioned by everything that already knows how to read
a bucket \(em and an application in any language carries it the same way.
.SH ACCESS LEVELS
Per kind, because the verbs differ. Anything else is refused, and the refusal
says what would have worked.
.TS
tab(@);
l l.
\fBqueues\fR@produce, consume
\fBtopics\fR@produce, subscribe
\fBbuckets\fR@read, write, subscribe
.TE
.PP
Each is granted narrowed to the one resource, through a role euclid writes named
.IR access\-<kind>\-<level> .
Producing and consuming are deliberately separate: the service that fills a queue
and the one that drains it are usually different, and each reaching only its own
end is the point.
.SH APPLYING IS A FULL RECONCILE
.B What the application owns is created; what it owned and no longer declares is
.BR deleted ;
its access grants are replaced with exactly the ones the file asks for.
.PP
A deleted queue takes its messages with it and a deleted bucket its objects. Only
a resource a
.I previous
declaration created is ever removed \(em the ERNs are recorded on the application,
so removing a line from the file is the only way to reach a deletion \(em and what
went is named in the answer rather than left to be noticed.
.PP
.B Run \-\-dry\-run first.
It prints the merged document and stops, uploading nothing and changing nothing.
.PP
Grants an operator made by hand are left alone: only the
.I access\-
roles this writes are replaced.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-application\-id " " \fINAME\fR
The application the declaration belongs to. Required. Its bucket is where the
declaration is stored, so the application has to exist already.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dir " " \fIFOLDER\fR
Folder of declaration files. Defaults to
.IR euclid .
Every
.I *.json
in it is read, in name order.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the merged declaration and stop.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The declaration was stored and applied, or printed for a dry run.
.TP
.B 1
The folder is missing or holds no JSON, a file could not be read or is not a
valid declaration, two files name the same resource, a
.B uses
entry names something that does not exist, the application is unknown, or the
server could not be reached.
.SH EXAMPLES
Look first:
.RS
.nf
cd file\-copy\-service
euclid\-cli eap deploy\-infrastructure \-n file\-copy \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.RE
.PP
Then apply it:
.RS
.nf
euclid\-cli eap deploy\-infrastructure \-n file\-copy
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-create-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap-redeploy-application (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eam-list-grants (1)
